# APML Playback: The consecutive-repeat law (A-64)
# SSI Learning App
# Version: 1.1.0
# Last updated: 2026-08-06
# Source: packages/player-vue/src/playback/capConsecutiveRepeats.ts
#         packages/player-vue/src/providers/generateLearningScript.ts
#         packages/player-vue/src/providers/backendCyclesToRounds.ts
#         packages/player-vue/src/providers/toSimpleRounds.ts
#         packages/player-vue/src/composables/usePodLapScheduler.ts
#         packages/player-vue/src/composables/useLayer1Scheduler.ts
#         packages/player-vue/src/composables/useOfflinePlay.ts

## ============================================================================
## THE LAW
## ============================================================================

SPECIFICATION:
  name: "No prompt three times in a row"
  ruling: |
    Tom, 2026-08-06, clearing plate item A-64, verbatim:

      "no mode should ever repeat the same prompt more than twice
       consecutively"

    It binds EVERY mode: the 4-phase learning player in Easy and Fast,
    Listening mode / the pods, infinite play, and every degraded or fallback
    playback path. Two identical prompts back to back is legal. Three is not,
    under any configuration.
  scope: |
    CONSECUTIVENESS ONLY. Spaced repetition is untouched — a phrase may be
    revisited any number of times across a session, and total repetition
    counts are preserved wherever the arithmetic allows. The fix for a third
    consecutive play is to RE-INTERLEAVE it (move it past a differing item),
    never to delete it. A rep is dropped only when there is genuinely nothing
    to interleave against.
  identity: |
    "The same prompt" = identical normalised known text paired with identical
    normalised target text (the getPhraseId notion). Listening items are the
    exception: a pod sentence play carries no known text and a pod translation
    carries no target text, so pod plays are identified by AUDIO ID — which
    makes 'ps' and 'ps2x' (the same clip at 1× and 2×) correctly the same
    prompt.

## ============================================================================
## THE ENFORCEMENT POINT
## ============================================================================

COMPONENT:
  file: "packages/player-vue/src/playback/capConsecutiveRepeats.ts"
  exports:
    - "capConsecutiveRepeats(items, identityOf, options) → { items, dropped, tail, reordered, forcedKeeps }"
    - "capRoundCycles(rounds, identityOf) → { rounds, dropped, reorderedRounds } — per-round, seam-seeded"
    - "cyclePromptIdentity(cycle) → string — prompt identity for a player Cycle"
    - "findConsecutiveBreach(items, identityOf, max) → index | -1"
  purity: "Pure. No I/O, no mutation of the input."
  algorithm: |
    Earliest legal item that keeps the remainder schedulable. Walk the queue
    and emit the first remaining item that neither creates a third consecutive
    repeat nor strands the tail. Input order survives except where the law
    forces a swap.

    The feasibility lookahead is load-bearing: an identity with c copies fits
    only if c <= max * (others + 1) - trailing_run. Plain first-legal-wins
    emits A,B,B out of A,B,B,A,B,B,B and then has to drop a B; the lookahead
    finds B,B,A,B,B,A,B and keeps all seven. With it, a rep is dropped only
    when NO ordering could have kept it.
  options:
    max: "Identical items allowed back to back. Default 2 — the law."
    seed: "Identities immediately preceding this sequence, so the cap holds across a boundary the caller processes in separate calls."
    minKeep: "Never return fewer than N items even if the law would empty the sequence. Keeping the session alive outranks the cap; such items are counted in forcedKeeps."

## ============================================================================
## APPLICATION SITES
## ============================================================================

SITE:
  name: "Main learning script + infinite play"
  file: "packages/player-vue/src/providers/generateLearningScript.ts"
  placement: |
    A final per-round pass over the playable item list, after the
    consecutive-duplicate dedup and the missing-text filter, before the
    round recount and return.
  round_confinement: |
    Re-interleaving NEVER crosses a round boundary — rounds are the player's
    unit of position. Each round is capped separately and seeded with the
    previous round's tail, so the law holds at the seam without an item ever
    migrating between rounds.
  identity_fn: "scriptItemIdentity (exported from the same module)"
  status: |
    A FLOOR, not a repair. The pre-existing consecutive-duplicate pass already
    collapses adjacent identical ordinary cycles to one, so the main loop and
    the infinite-play tail were already lawful — verified by removing the cap
    and watching the A-64 regression tests still pass. The cap exists so that
    no future emitter, no algorithm_config script_shape value (Easy mode's
    doubled n1PhraseCount included) and no listening item type can breach.

SITE:
  name: "The round adapters — the true last word for the main player"
  files:
    - "packages/player-vue/src/providers/backendCyclesToRounds.ts (backendCyclesToRounds + infPlayCyclesToRounds)"
    - "packages/player-vue/src/providers/toSimpleRounds.ts"
  placement: "capRoundCycles() on the returned Round[], immediately before SimplePlayer receives them."
  why_here: |
    INSTANT_PLAYBACK_ALL is true in LearningPlayer.vue, so EVERY course takes
    the instant-playback path: the server cycles route → useInstantPlayback →
    backendCyclesToRounds → SimplePlayer. That path never touches
    generateLearningScript, so a cap living only in the generator protects
    nobody on the live default path. The adapters are the one point both paths
    pass through.

    Placing it here also survives the adapters' own missing-audio filtering,
    which drops unplayable cycles and can therefore pull two previously
    separated prompts together.
  covers_server_route: |
    api/courses/[code]/cycles.ts emits `intro` then `debut` with IDENTICAL
    known_text + target_text (buildLegoCycles reads lego.known_text /
    legoTargets.target_text for both) and then appends build phrases with no
    adjacency check — so a first BUILD phrase duplicating the bare LEGO text
    gives three in a row. Capping client-side covers every consumer of that
    route without a second, drifting server-side implementation.

SITE:
  name: "Layer-1 listening cups"
  file: "packages/player-vue/src/composables/useLayer1Scheduler.ts"
  placement: "buildSeedPlays(), on the returned plays, identified by audio id."
  breach_it_closes: |
    DEFAULT_SEED_PLAYLIST (t1·known·t2·t1) is lawful — the trailing t2·t1 pair
    is two plays of the same clip, which the law allows. But the playlist is
    admin-tunable via algorithm_config['listening'].seedPlaylist with no
    validation, so a saved ['known','t1','t1','t2'] would emit three identical
    target plays. The cap makes that unreachable from config.

SITE:
  name: "Listening mode pod laps"
  file: "packages/player-vue/src/composables/usePodLapScheduler.ts"
  placement: "nextLap(), on the composed play list, after buildMainStage."
  breach_it_closes: |
    THE real breach. A pod whose active cohorts hold a single sentence emits a
    playlist that ends on that clip (stage 8 = ['ps2x','ps2x']) and the next
    lap restarts on the same clip. Verified three-in-a-row without the cap.
  cross_lap: |
    The previous lap's tail is carried into the next lap as `seed`, because
    laps run back to back. It is NOT carried when bookends are present — the
    intro/outro clips genuinely separate the two laps.
  degenerate_lap: |
    minKeep: 1. A one-clip lap arriving on top of a tail that already holds
    two of it is reduced rather than emptied, and the shortfall is logged. A
    lap never returns zero plays.
  configuration_independence: |
    Applied downstream of resolveStageConfig, so an admin-saved stage playlist
    in algorithm_config.pods cannot breach the law.

SITE:
  name: "Offline degradation ladder"
  file: "packages/player-vue/src/composables/useOfflinePlay.ts"
  placement: "getNextPlayableCycle(), across all three degradation levels."
  behaviour: |
    A cycle that has already played twice in a row is excluded from the
    scheduled pick and from the belt-only pool while any other cached cycle
    exists. Level 3 ('repeat') is the one place the law yields: with literally
    one cached cycle, keeping the session alive wins, and the breach is logged
    as a warning rather than passing silently.

## ============================================================================
## KNOWN RESIDUE — audited, deliberately not capped
## ============================================================================

RESIDUE:
  simpleplayer_resume:
    file: "packages/player-vue/src/playback/SimplePlayer.ts (resume())"
    finding: |
      resume() deliberately restarts the CURRENT cycle from the prompt phase —
      "if the learner has stopped the app at all, we give them the full 4-phase
      cycle from the top". Construction: cycles N and N+1 carry the same text
      (legal, two in a row); the learner pauses part-way through N+1 and
      resumes; the prompt sounds a third time. PlayerConductor can reach the
      same path automatically when an interlude or seek times out or throws.
    decision: |
      NOT capped. A queue-level cap cannot see it — only a resume-time check
      could — and changing resume semantics to chase an interaction-dependent
      edge risks a worse regression than the edge itself. Logged for Tom.
  stage0_and_atom_fusion:
    files:
      - "packages/player-vue/src/composables/usePodStage0.ts + @ssi/core stage0Sequence.ts"
      - "packages/player-vue/src/composables/usePodAtomFusion.ts"
    finding: |
      buildLadder's visits loop can construct three in a row at a visit
      boundary when visits >= 2. usePodAtomFusion has no caller outside its own
      test.
    decision: |
      NOT capped — unreachable from learner-facing playback. Stage 0 was retired
      from the runtime 2026-07-14 and survives only behind the admin route
      /admin/pod-auditioner. Revisit if either is ever re-wired into playback.

## ============================================================================
## TESTS
## ============================================================================

TESTS:
  - file: "packages/player-vue/src/playback/capConsecutiveRepeats.test.ts"
    covers: |
      Unit behaviour plus a 400-case property sweep: output never contains
      three consecutive equal identities, nothing is invented or duplicated,
      and totals are preserved whenever max*(others+1) allows.
  - file: "packages/player-vue/src/providers/a64ConsecutivePromptCap.test.ts"
    covers: |
      The A-64 case directly — Easy-mode script shape (n1PhraseCount 6,
      useConsolidationCount 4, maxBuildPhrases 14, maxSpacedRepPhrases 24)
      against USE pools of 1, 2 and 3 phrases, for the main loop and the
      infinite-play tail, plus the default (Fast) shape.
  - file: "packages/player-vue/src/providers/a64RoundAdapters.test.ts"
    covers: |
      The round adapters — backendCyclesToRounds, infPlayCyclesToRounds and
      toSimpleRounds — against the intro/debut/build triple the server cycles
      route makes possible, within a round and across the round seam. Three of
      these five tests fail without the cap.
  - file: "packages/player-vue/src/composables/usePodLapScheduler.a64.test.ts"
    covers: |
      Pod laps with 1, 2 and 4 sentences, with and without bookends, flattened
      across consecutive laps into the sequence the learner actually hears.

